{"authored" [{"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}+6t-3⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the times at which the particle is stationary." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b3a5f891d7c29d5c23ea696b3c05384bff60f137dd2f817d72bf6fea00150c5" "basis" 1448 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-3,4t+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3({t}^{2}-1),2(2t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3(t-1)(t+1),2(2t+3)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(t-1)(t+1)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ 2(2t+3)=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is no value of $t$ that satisfies both equations, so there are no stationary points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b3a5f891d7c29d5c23ea696b3c05384bff60f137dd2f817d72bf6fea00150c5" "basis" 1448 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040567/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-31654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_c